window.examShortData = [{"id": "hsc2025_q18b", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [15], "marks": 4, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain ONE trend in global biodiversity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 18(b)):\n4 marks - Explains ONE trend in global biodiversity\n3 marks - Describes ONE trend in global biodiversity\n2 marks - Outlines ONE trend in global biodiversity\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: One trend in global biodiversity is the rapid decline in wildlife populations, with a dramatic decrease especially in the last 50 years. This trend is driven by habitat loss, climate change, pollution, overexploitation, and invasive species. Habitat destruction, particularly from agriculture and urbanisation, fragments ecosystems, making it difficult for species to survive resulting in population decline. Climate change makes these effects worse by altering habitats and food sources."}, {"id": "hsc2025_q19a", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [34, 35], "marks": 4, "directive": "Account for", "question": "Account for the identity of ONE place within a larger urban settlement.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 19(a)):\n4 marks - Accounts for the identity of ONE place within a larger urban settlement\n3 marks - Describes the identity of ONE place within a larger urban settlement\n2 marks - Outlines the identity of ONE place within a larger urban settlement\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: The Plateau-Mont-Royal neighbourhood in Montreal has developed a vibrant arts scene, colourful murals, and historic architecture. It evolved from a working-class area to a trendy neighbourhood, due to its proximity to the city centre, attracting artists, young professionals, and tourists. The area's mix of cafes, boutiques, and green spaces like Parc La Fontaine, contribute to this identity, and make it a lively and culturally rich part of this French-speaking city."}, {"id": "hsc2025_q19b", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [31], "marks": 5, "directive": "Analyse", "question": "Analyse how ONE place in a rural setting is linked to other places.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 19(b)):\n5 marks - Analyses how ONE place in a rural setting is linked to other places\n4 marks - Describes how ONE place in a rural setting is linked to other places\n3 marks - Outlines how ONE place in a rural setting is linked to other places\n2 marks - Identifies how ONE place in a rural setting is linked to other places\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Mudgee in central western NSW is linked to other places through various connections. Its agricultural products, particularly wine, are exported to major urban centres like Sydney and Melbourne, leading to economic growth, jobs and population increase. The town is also connected through transportation networks, including the Castlereagh Highway, which facilitate trade and tourism. Additionally, Mudgee's cultural links are strengthened through regional events like the Mudgee Food and Wine Festival, attracting visitors from across Australia. Environmental links, such as the Goulburn River, support local agriculture and regional water systems. These connections help shape Mudgee's identity and development."}, {"id": "hsc2025_q20a", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [3], "marks": 3, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the role of global forums in achieving global sustainability.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 20(a)):\n3 marks - Describes the role of global forums in achieving global sustainability\n2 marks - Outlines the role of global forums in achieving global sustainability\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Global forums help international collaboration by creating time and space to discuss issues and set shared sustainability goals. They provide a platform for countries to align policies, share best practices, and commit to environmental targets to address issues like climate change and poverty reduction."}, {"id": "hsc2025_q20b", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [7], "marks": 5, "directive": "Discuss", "question": "Answer parts (b) and (c) with reference to a global economic activity you have studied. Discuss the effect of ONE influence on the global economic activity identified.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 20(b)):\n5 marks - Discusses the effect of ONE influence on a global economic activity\n4 marks - Explains the effect of ONE influence on a global economic activity\n3 marks - Outlines the effect of ONE influence on a global economic activity\n2 marks - Describes an influence on a global economic activity\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer:\nActivity: Mining\nTechnology advances significantly influence global mining operations. Innovations in automation, remote sensing and AI can improve efficiency of production and resource use resulting in higher profits and growth in the activity. However, these innovations have high initial costs and investment which favour larger mining companies in less regulated countries."}, {"id": "hsc2025_q20c", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [10], "marks": 5, "directive": "Compare", "question": "Compare TWO strategies for achieving sustainability in the global economic activity identified.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 20(c)):\n5 marks - Provides a thorough comparison of TWO strategies for achieving sustainability in a global economic activity\n4 marks - Provides some comparison of TWO strategies for achieving sustainability in a global economic activity\n3 marks - Outlines TWO strategies for achieving sustainability in a global economic activity\n2 marks - Identifies TWO strategies or outlines ONE strategy for achieving sustainability in a global economic activity\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: One strategy for sustainability in the mining industry is utilising more efficient technologies to recycle materials, such as metals and minerals, to reduce the need for new extraction and therefore reduce resource use. This helps conserve natural resources and lowers environmental impact. Another strategy is reducing energy consumption by using renewable energy sources like solar or wind power in mining operations. Both approaches aim to minimise environmental harm, but recycling focuses on resource conservation, while energy reduction targets lowering emissions and energy costs."}, {"id": "hsc2025_q21", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [28, 1], "marks": 6, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "The diagram shows the four pillars of sustainability: Social, Economic, Environmental, Cultural. With reference to TWO pillars of sustainability, evaluate the success of ONE initiative or project used in the management of rural and/or urban places.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 21, band marking):\n6 marks - Demonstrates an extensive understanding of sustainable management of a rural or urban place with reference to TWO pillars of sustainability; Makes a comprehensive judgement of the success of ONE initiative or project using the pillars of sustainability\n5 marks - Demonstrates a thorough understanding of sustainable management of a rural or urban place with reference to at least ONE pillar of sustainability; Makes a judgement of the success of ONE initiative or project using the pillars of sustainability\n3-4 marks - Demonstrates sound understanding of sustainable management of a rural or urban place; Outlines the sustainability of ONE initiative or project\n2 marks - Demonstrates a basic understanding of sustainable management or of an initiative or project\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: The Green Exchange in the regional centre of Curitiba, Brazil has proven to be a successful initiative in sustainably managing an urban setting. Environmentally, the program reduces illegal dumping and increases recycling by allowing residents to exchange recyclable materials for fresh produce. Economically, it supports regional agriculture by creating a market for local farmers and reducing reliance on large industry. The program overall therefore supports the area to thrive economically and improve urban cleanliness."}, {"id": "hsc2025_q22a", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [40], "marks": 3, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Answer parts (a)\u2013(c) with reference to ONE large city of 5 million people or more, outside Australia, that you have studied. Outline the character of this city.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 22(a)):\n3 marks - Outlines the character of a suitable city\n2 marks - Identifies features of a suitable city\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Tokyo is a dynamic and densely populated city that blends advanced technology with tradition. Known for its efficiency, cleanliness, and safety, it offers a unique mix of modern skyscrapers, historic temples, and vibrant street life. Despite its fast pace, Tokyo maintains a strong sense of community, making it both a global business hub and a culturally rich, liveable city."}, {"id": "hsc2025_q22b", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [42], "marks": 5, "directive": "Describe", "question": "With reference to the same large city of 5 million people or more, outside Australia, that you have studied: Describe TWO challenges of living in this city.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 22(b)):\n5 marks - Describes TWO challenges of living in the city\n4 marks - Describes ONE challenge of living in the city and outlines ONE other\n3 marks - Outlines TWO challenges of living in the city\n2 marks - Outlines a challenge of living in the city OR Identifies challenges of living in the city\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: One major challenge of living in Tokyo is the difficulty faced by families and low-income individuals in finding suitable housing due to the high cost and limited size of housing. In central areas like Minato, monthly rent for a one-bedroom apartment can exceed 200 000 JPY. Due to land scarcity, many residents live in units under 25 square metres with minimal storage and living space. Another significant challenge is overcrowding, especially on public transport. Train lines like the Tozai line often run at over 200% capacity during peak hours, meaning passengers are packed in with no personal space. This daily stress impacts physical comfort and mental health, particularly for older adults and people with disabilities."}, {"id": "hsc2025_q22c", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [43], "marks": 12, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "With reference to the same large city of 5 million people or more, outside Australia, that you have studied: Evaluate the effectiveness of TWO strategies to improve people's quality of life in this city.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 22(c), band marking):\n11-12 marks - Demonstrates comprehensive knowledge and understanding of TWO strategies to improve people's quality of life in the city; Provides a detailed judgement about the effectiveness of strategies to improve people's quality of life in the city; Integrates relevant examples, geographical information and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate to support the response; Presents a sustained, logical and cohesive response using appropriate geographical ideas, terms and concepts\n8-10 marks - Demonstrates well-developed knowledge and understanding of TWO strategies to improve people's quality of life in the city; Provides some judgement about the effectiveness of strategies to improve people's quality of life in the city; Refers to relevant examples, geographical information and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate to support the response; Presents a logical response using appropriate geographical terminology and concepts\n5-7 marks - Demonstrates sound knowledge and understanding of TWO strategies to improve people's quality of life in the city; Describes strategies for improving people's quality of life in the city; Refers to examples, geographical information and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate; Presents a structured response using appropriate geographical concepts\n3-4 marks - Demonstrates some knowledge and understanding of people's quality of life; May refer to examples, geographical information and/or the Stimulus Booklet; Uses some geographical terminology\n1-2 marks - Demonstrates a basic understanding of quality of life\n\nAnswers could include:\n- Wellbeing strategies such as: social infrastructure and services, mixed income development, walkable cities, community initiatives\n- Urban planning strategies such as: housing, greenspace, public transport, congestion reduction, recycling, air quality improvement, stormwater, sanitation\n- Economic development strategies such as: employment schemes, local business support, micro-credit and other NGO support, incubator hubs\n- Resilience strategies such as: climate change adaptations, disaster preparedness, crime prevention\n- Evaluation could be conducted via criteria such as safety, equity, quality of daily life and environmental health."}, {"id": "sample_17a", "source": "Sample Exam", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [17], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the role that can be played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems.", "markingGuideline": "Official marking guideline (top band, 4 marks): Provides characteristics and features of the role that can be played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems. (3 marks: provides SOME characteristics and features; 2 marks: demonstrates a basic understanding; 1 mark: provides some relevant information.) Official sample answer: Many Indigenous cultures have strong connections with, and a deep understanding of, the land and how to manage and care for it, which stem from knowledge passed down for thousands of years. Indigenous Peoples can play an important role in the contemporary management of ecosystems through consultation and joint decision-making. Indigenous Peoples' knowledges and approaches can be incorporated into contemporary management strategies to enhance sustainability. Strategies such as closed seasons, sustained burning and having protected species are examples of Indigenous Peoples' customary management approaches which have been adapted into contemporary ecosystem management."}, {"id": "sample_17b", "source": "Sample Exam", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [3], "marks": 4, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Many medicines have been developed using Indigenous Peoples' knowledges of the medicinal properties of plants.\n\nExplain how benefit sharing presents opportunities for the sustainable production of plant-based medicines.", "markingGuideline": "Official marking guideline (top band, 4 marks): Demonstrates a thorough understanding of how benefit sharing presents opportunities for the sustainable production of plant-based medicines. (3 marks: sound understanding; 2 marks: basic understanding; 1 mark: some relevant information.) Official sample answer: Benefit sharing agreements provide for the ongoing and equitable distribution of benefits arising from the sharing of knowledges, practices and resources. Indigenous Peoples' sharing of knowledges and practices with research institutions or medical companies could result in advancements in treatments and an increased awareness of sustainable production methods to ensure ongoing availability of raw materials. Research institutions could learn from Indigenous Peoples' methods of cultivating plants and transferring raw materials into treatments, and medical companies could share production technologies to enhance efficiency. Economic benefits of these agreements could include supply contracts and corporate partnerships."}, {"id": "sample_18", "source": "Sample Exam", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [28], "marks": 6, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how ONE initiative or project has led to sustainable management of a rural or urban place.", "markingGuideline": "Official marking guideline (top band, 6 marks): Demonstrates a comprehensive understanding of sustainable management of a rural or urban place; clearly explains how ONE initiative or project has led to sustainable management. (Lower bands, 5-1 marks, require progressively less thorough understanding/explanation, down to 1 mark for providing some relevant information.) Official sample answer: Boco Rock Wind Farm, in Nimmitabel NSW, is an example of a project which has led to sustainable management as it provides clean and sustainable energy for this community. Stage One of the wind farm consists of 67 turbines, generating 113 MW of renewable energy. The turbines capture the kinetic energy of the wind and convert it into electricity as the shaft drives the generator, thus creating sustainable energy production. Boco Rock Wind Farm also uses a community engagement strategy that attempts to make a contribution to the local and wider community. It provides employment and infrastructure, such as new roads, to the rural community. The project is delivering clean and sustainable energy to the local area. This is an example of sustainable management, as it provides the energy needs for communities and lowers the ecological footprint."}, {"id": "sample_19a", "source": "Sample Exam", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [34], "marks": 3, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how ONE spatial technology tool could be used in a geographical inquiry into an urban place.", "markingGuideline": "Official marking guideline (top band, 3 marks): Correctly identifies a spatial technology tool; provides a detailed explanation of how AND why the spatial technology tool would be used during a geographical inquiry. (2 marks: identifies tool and describes how OR why it would be used; 1 mark: provides some relevant information.) Official sample answer: To begin the inquiry, Google Earth Pro could be used to zoom in and view at different scales \u2013 for example from citywide, to a suburb or community and then to street level. This would enable the identification of physical and human characteristics such as the location of a suburb or precinct in a city, building density, facilities and services. The tools in Google Earth could be used to measure distances, to calculate area and identify changes over time."}, {"id": "sample_19b", "source": "Sample Exam", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [34], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe TWO appropriate fieldwork techniques to use in collecting data or information on the human characteristics of urban places.", "markingGuideline": "Official marking guideline (top band, 4 marks): Describes TWO appropriate fieldwork techniques that could be used to collect data or information on human characteristics of urban places. (3 marks: describes one technique in detail and outlines a second; 2 marks: outlines two techniques OR describes one in detail; 1 mark: some relevant information.) Official sample answer: One fieldwork technique could be conducting a survey to gather information from residents of the place. Survey questions can provide quantitative human data such as age, gender, type of employment and distance travelled to work and qualitative information such as how safe they feel, access to services and facilities, opinions about liveability, happiness and issues of concern. Quantitative data can be graphed. Another technique could be taking photographs at different locations to illustrate human characteristics of an urban place such as transport infrastructure, types of housing, shops and facilities such as schools and hospitals. Human challenges such as urban decay can be captured in photographs."}, {"id": "sample_20b", "source": "Sample Exam", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [9], "marks": 5, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain potential impacts of the global fast fashion industry on either aquatic or land-based ecosystems.", "markingGuideline": "Official marking guideline (top band, 5 marks): Demonstrates a thorough understanding of how and why the fast fashion industry could impact ecosystems; relates impacts to aquatic OR land-based ecosystems in detail. (Lower bands, 4-1 marks, require progressively more basic/limited understanding.) Official sample answer (land-based ecosystems): The high volume of fast fashion waste increases demand for landfill sites and creates pressure to clear land to produce more fibre to satisfy increasing demand for fashion. These activities contribute to the loss of terrestrial ecosystems such as forests, grasslands and freshwater wetlands, reducing their spatial extent and increasing their vulnerability to further change. Ecosystems fragmented by land clearing, such as eucalypt forests, are more vulnerable to the edge effects of settlement and agriculture such as exotic species and feral animals. Waste in landfill releases methane, a greenhouse gas, which, along with the CO2 produced by production and transportation, affects ecosystem functioning by altering the physical conditions in which an ecosystem can survive."}, {"id": "sample_20c", "source": "Sample Exam", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [11], "marks": 6, "directive": "With reference to a global economic activity you have studied, analyse", "question": "With reference to a global economic activity you have studied, analyse ONE strategy used to achieve social or environmental sustainability.", "markingGuideline": "Official marking guideline (top band, 6 marks): Makes clear reference to a global economic activity; clearly draws out and relates implications of ONE strategy used to achieve social or environmental sustainability. (Lower bands, 5-1 marks, require progressively less developed reference/analysis, down to identifying/describing only.) Official sample answer: Cruise ship tourism threatens the environmental sustainability of marine ecosystems through its impact on biodiversity. In Glacier Bay National Park in Alaska, USA tourists come to see wildlife whose survival depends on a healthy marine environment. Pollution from wastewater and ballast discharge, fuel leakage and biocides from ships, as well as the noise, lights, and physical disturbance of wildlife and habitats by tourists needed effective management. The US government agency managing cruise shipping introduced limited entry permits to reduce environmental impacts and increase compliance with environmental regulations such as waste disposal. Every ten years, cruise operators must submit a proposal to visit. The strategy has been effective because new contracts are awarded to operators with a record of following environmental regulations. As a result of intense competition for limited permits, cruise tourism in Alaska has become more environmentally sustainable."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q17a", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [7], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Answer parts (a) and (b) with reference to ONE global economic activity you have studied. Global economic activity: ______\n(a) Outline ONE influence on the global economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (2 marks): Correctly identifies a global economic activity and accurately outlines ONE influence on this activity - 2 marks; Provides some relevant information related to influences on the global economic activity - 1 mark. Other influences could include: Biophysical; Economic; Technological; Political/organisational. Sample Answer (Global Economic Activity: Tourism): Technology plays a significant role in influencing the global economic activity of tourism. Information technology has transformed how tourists plan, book, and experience their trips. Online booking platforms like Expedia and Booking.com simplify the reservation process, while mobile apps such as Google Maps and TripAdvisor guide tourists in planning itineraries, navigating destinations, and finding top attractions or dining options."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q17b", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [8], "marks": 3, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Answer parts (a) and (b) with reference to ONE global economic activity you have studied. Global economic activity: ______\n(b) Describe ONE current trend of the global economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (3 marks): Provides a detailed description of one current trend on the global economic activity - 3 marks; Provides some characteristics and features of a current trend in the global economic activity - 2 marks; Provides some relevant information related to a trend - 1 mark. Sample Answer: A current trend in global tourism is wellness travel. According to National Geographic, 21% of travelers in 2022 were motivated by health or wellness-related reasons. This trend is expected to continue, with Expedia predicting a 30% increase in demand, particularly for wellness retreats. These retreats cater to a wide range of needs. For example, Foma in Byron Bay offers yoga, meditation, and ayurvedic treatments with plant-based meals. In contrast, the Sofitel Hotel in Darling Harbour, Sydney, provides a half-day wellness journey, combining exercise classes with pampering treatments, such as a Biologique Recherche facial or infrared sauna. Wellness travel is an emerging trend in the global tourism industry."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q18a", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [16], "marks": 2, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe ONE conservation program that has successfully managed an ecosystem.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (2 marks): Provides the features and characteristics of ONE conservation program that has successfully managed an ecosystem - 2 marks; Sketches in general terms, some features and/or characteristics of ONE conservation program that has successfully managed an ecosystem - 1 mark. Answers could include: The GBRMPA conserves the Great Barrier Reef by zoning to limit harmful activities, promoting sustainable tourism, monitoring reef health, addressing coral bleaching, and improving water quality to preserve biodiversity. The Badu ITW in Sydney Olympic Park is conserved through habitat restoration, invasive species control, and tidal flow management. Sustainable public access via boardwalks balances education with ecosystem protection, enhancing biodiversity and ecological health."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q18b", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [17], "marks": 4, "directive": "Examine", "question": "Examine the role played by Indigenous peoples in contemporary ecosystem management practices.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (4 marks): Provides a detailed inquiry into the role played by Indigenous Peoples in more than one contemporary ecosystem management practice, and provides relevant examples to support the response - 4 marks; Provides an inquiry into the role played by Indigenous Peoples in contemporary ecosystem management practices, may provide a relevant example - 3 marks; Provides general information about the role played by Indigenous Peoples in ecosystem management practices, may only provide information on one management practice - 2 marks; Provides relevant information - 1 mark. Answers could include: Indigenous Peoples play a key role in ecosystem management by combining traditional knowledge with modern practices. For example, the Firestick Alliance in Australia uses controlled burns to reduce bushfire risks and support biodiversity. Similarly, Kaitiakitanga in New Zealand sees Maori communities manage the Whanganui River as a living entity, ensuring environmental sustainability and cultural connection. These approaches demonstrate how Indigenous knowledge enhances conservation efforts."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q18c", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [13], "marks": 5, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain the value of ecosystems and biodiversity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (5 marks): Demonstrated deep knowledge and understanding of the value of ecosystems and biodiversity, supports answer with a range of relevant examples - 5 marks; Provides a general understanding of the value of ecosystems and biodiversity, may provide a relevant example - 4 marks; Describes some of the values of ecosystems and biodiversity - 3 marks; Provides some basic understanding of the value of ecosystems and biodiversity - 2 marks; Provides some relevant information - 1 mark. Answers could include: Ecosystems and biodiversity are essential for maintaining ecological balance, supporting human well-being, and providing vital services such as clean air, water, and food. For example, the Amazon Rainforest is a global carbon sink, regulating climate and supporting countless species. Similarly, coral reefs, such as the Great Barrier Reef, protect coastlines and provide habitat for marine life. Biodiversity also supports agriculture, with species such as bees playing a crucial role in pollination. Protecting ecosystems ensures the survival of these services, highlighting their intrinsic and economic value."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q19a", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [29], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline the location and character of ONE rural place. Rural place: ______", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (2 marks): Sketches in general terms the location and character of a rural place - 2 marks; Provides some relevant information about the location and/or character of a rural place - 1 mark. Sample Answer: Bellingen is a small town on the Mid North Coast of New South Wales, positioned midway between Sydney and Brisbane, located at 30 degrees 27' S, 152 degrees 54' E. The town is situated on Gumbaynggirr land and is divided by the Bellinger River. European settlers initially established timber logging and farming in the area, which shaped its agricultural character. The community's alternative lifestyle has supported creative industries and promoted organic and regenerative farming practices. As of the 2021 ABS census, Bellingen has a population of 3,201. The town's unique character attracts many tourists to its festivals and markets."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q19b", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [36], "marks": 4, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain TWO links that ONE urban place has to other places. Urban place: ______", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (4 marks): Demonstrates a thorough understanding of the cause of TWO links that this urban place has to other places, relates impacts to other places - 4 marks; Demonstrates a sound understanding of the cause of TWO links that this urban place has to other places, relates impacts to other places - 3 marks; Demonstrates a basic understanding of the link/s that this urban place has to other places, may relate some impacts to other places - 2 marks; Provides some relevant information - 1 mark. Answers could include categories: Cultural; Economic; Political. Sample Answer (Sydney Olympic Park): 1) Sydney 2000 Olympics - Sydney Olympic Park will always have a global link with the rest of the world as the host of the XXVII Olympics in 2000. During September 2000, Sydney welcomed 10,651 athletes from 199 countries who competed in 300 events. The Sydney 2000 Olympic Games was the most watched sport event at that time with more than 3.7 billion people tuned in to watch, which represents a 20% increase over the 1996 Atlanta Olympic Games. Sydney 2000 was broadcast in 220 countries and generated more than 36.1 billion television viewing hours. Therefore establishing a global cultural link with the rest of the world. 2) Business Links - As part of the redevelopment of SOP after the Olympics a key driver was to establish businesses within the precinct, however it took a number of years for it to gain traction. The first key businesses that were established were hotels with the Novotel, Pullman and Ibis groups having hotels within SOP which caters for tourists coming to see sporting or entertainment events. As a result of increased residential development throughout the precinct many cafes, restaurants and small businesses have developed to support the growing population within SOP. The area now has major businesses both government (NSW Ambulance Head Office) and private businesses such as Thales Australia. The NSW Government has committed to a Master Plan to further revitalise the precinct that will bring the jobs total to 34,000 and increase new homes to 10,700 for its 23,500 residents by 2030. Around 12,000 people currently work in the area in more than 200 businesses which is set to increase to more than 20,000 jobs by 2036."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q19c", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [43], "marks": 5, "directive": "Analyse", "question": "Analyse ONE response to a challenge experienced by ONE large city outside Australia, where the response has enhanced the city's sustainability. Large city outside of Australia: ______", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (5 marks): Makes clear reference to a large city, clearly draws out and relates implications of ONE response to a challenge that enhances the sustainability in this large city - 5 marks; Makes reference to a large city, relates implications of ONE response to a challenge that enhances the sustainability in this large city - 4 marks; Identifies a large city, attempts to relate implications of ONE response to a challenge that enhances the sustainability in this large city - 3 marks; Identifies a large city, describes ONE response to a challenge that enhances the sustainability in this large city - 2 marks; Provides some relevant information - 1 mark. Examples of large cities outside of Australia: Singapore, London, Hanoi, Nairobi, Santiago, Mumbai, Paris. Challenges could include: Rapid population growth; Access to services and facilities; Affordability; Liveability; Ecological footprint; Climate change. Responses to challenges including strategies to enhance the city's sustainability such as improving people's quality of life and reducing spatial inequality. Nairobi responses: Access to health services - the Linda Mama program offers free maternity services to pregnant women, ensuring that even those in low-income areas have access to essential healthcare services. Links to UN SDGs Goals 3, 5 and 10. Public Transportation Improvements - the development of the Nairobi Metropolitan Area Transport Authority (NaMATA) and the introduction of the Bus Rapid Transit (BRT) system are designed to enhance public transportation, reduce traffic congestion, and provide affordable and efficient transit options for residents. Links to UN SDGs Goals 3, 9, 11 and 13. Singapore responses: Public Housing - The Housing and Development Board (HDB) appointed by the Minister for National Development. Provide affordable housing to the majority of Singaporeans. Over 80% of the population lives in HDB flats, which are well-integrated with amenities like schools, parks, and public transportation. Links to UN SDGs Goals 3, 10 and 11."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q21a", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [18], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Select ONE ecosystem from Australia OR ONE ecosystem from outside Australia. Using the selected ecosystem, answer Question 21.\n(a) Describe the characteristics of the ecosystem, including its spatial pattern and the nature of its biodiversity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (4 marks): Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem from Australia OR ONE ecosystem from outside of Australia, detailed description of the characteristics of the ecosystem, including its spatial pattern and the nature of its biodiversity - 4 marks; Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem, describes the characteristics of the ecosystem, including its spatial pattern and the nature of its biodiversity - 3 marks; Identifies ONE ecosystem, outlines features and some characteristics of the ecosystem - 2 marks; Provides some relevant information - 1 mark. Sample Answers - Australian Ecosystems: Coral Reef-Great Barrier Reef; Intertidal Wetlands-Homebush Bay, Towra Point; Alpine Ecosystem-Kosciuszko National Park. Outside Australia: Wetlands-Florida Everglades; Fiordland-New Zealand; Okavango Delta. Sample paragraph: The Coral reef ecosystem of the Great Barrier Reef is the largest coral ecosystem in the world. Located off the east coast of Queensland it extends for 2300km between 8 degrees S and 24 degrees S and covers an area of 344,000km2. The coral reef ecosystem of the Great Barrier Reef is one of the most biologically diverse ecosystems in the world, with over 400 species of coral and 1625 species of fish found within the reef."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q21b", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [20], "marks": 6, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Select ONE ecosystem from Australia OR ONE ecosystem from outside Australia. Using the selected ecosystem, answer Question 21.\n(b) Explain how ONE human-induced modification influences this ecosystem.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (6 marks): Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem from Australia OR ONE ecosystem from outside of Australia, detailed explanation of how ONE human induced modification influences the ecosystem - 6 marks; Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem, explains how ONE human induced modification influences the ecosystem - 4-5 marks; Identifies ONE ecosystem, describes ONE human induced modification of the ecosystem - 2-3 marks; Provides basic information on an ecosystem or human modifications - 1 mark. Answers could include: Coral Reefs: climate change - increases water temperatures which decrease coral functioning and leads to coral bleaching. Alpine: tourism - construction of ski industry has disrupted the biodiversity of the area causing permanent modifications with the ecosystem. Fiordland: introduced species - introduced species such as the Tahr goat eat large amounts of the natural vegetation and trample the natural vegetation causing damage."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q21c", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [21], "marks": 10, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "Select ONE ecosystem from Australia OR ONE ecosystem from outside Australia. Using the selected ecosystem, answer Question 21.\n(c) Evaluate TWO responses and/or strategies that have been used to maintain ecosystem functioning.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (10 marks, band descriptors): Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem, detailed judgment of TWO responses and/or strategies that have been used to maintain ecosystem functioning - 9-10 marks; Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem, clear judgment of TWO responses and/or strategies - 7-8 marks; Clearly identifies ONE ecosystem, some judgment of TWO responses and/or strategies - 5-6 marks; Identifies ONE ecosystem, provides some reasoning of ONE/TWO responses and/or strategies - 3-4 marks; Makes some reference to an ecosystem and/or responses and/or strategies - 1-2 marks. Answers could include (Coral Reef): 1) Zoning - has been a positive strategy in maintaining the function of the coral ecosystem because it ensures that high risk areas have been designated as restricted zones so that humans can't access these areas. The current Zoning Plan, introduced in 2004, is helping secure a better future for the GBR while supporting the vital social and economic benefits gained from Reef related tourism and recreation, commercial activities, and fishing. However, while Zoning has been effective in managing human use of the GBR it is ineffective against problems such as climate change and coral bleaching as the zones can't restrict the spread of warmer water throughout the GBR. 2) The Reef 2050 Water Quality Improvement Plan - was developed in 2017 to provide long-term management of water quality across the reef ecosystem. It covers not just the water on the reef itself but also importantly deals with the land that makes up the river catchments that eventually flow out onto the reef. The aim of the plan is to reduce 80% of dissolved nitrogen and 50% of sediment in the waters going into the reef from land-based catchments. This improvement plan has had moderate success with a small improvement across the whole area in water quality however there has been a much higher success with the reduction in pesticides across the area showing that the plan has achieved some levels of success."}, {"id": "indep2025_q18a", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [2], "marks": 2, "directive": "Define", "question": "Define the precautionary principle, providing an example.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Accurately defines, using an example, the precautionary principle (2 marks); Provides some relevant information (1 mark). Sample answer: The precautionary principle involves taking early action to prevent environmental harm, even if scientific evidence is not fully certain. In a kelp forest ecosystem like the Great Southern Reef, authorities might limit coastal development or ban certain fishing practices if there's a risk they could damage kelp beds, even before conclusive evidence proves the harm."}, {"id": "indep2025_q18b", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [5, 9], "marks": 4, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Refer to your knowledge of ONE global economic activity to answer Questions 18(b)-(c). Explain how criteria can be used to evaluate the sustainability of the economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Clearly explains how criteria can be used to evaluate the sustainability of an economic activity; shows a clear understanding of global sustainability (4 marks); Describes how criteria can be used to evaluate the sustainability of an economic activity (3 marks); Outlines criteria that can be used to evaluate the sustainability of an economic activity (2 marks); Provides some relevant information (1 mark). Sample answer: One form of criteria to evaluate the sustainability of cotton farming are the Sustainable Development Goals (SDG's). For example, SDG 12 'responsible consumption and production' could be used to determine the economic and environmental sustainability of cotton farming as it could measure the amount of waste in the primary phase of production. It could also be used to determine the amount of cotton that goes to landfill when producing textiles, which could result in the industry implementing a circular economy to minimise its ecological footprint and improve its reputation. Others could include: Carbon footprint; Water footprint; Pillars of sustainability (SEEC); Principles of ecologically sustainable development (ESD); Certification; Circular economy."}, {"id": "indep2025_q18c", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [6], "marks": 5, "directive": "Account for", "question": "Account for the nature and spatial patterns of the economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Clearly provides reasons for the nature and spatial patterns of the global economic activity; demonstrates deep knowledge and understanding of an economic activity (5 marks); Attempts to provide reasons for the nature and spatial patterns of the global economic activity; demonstrates knowledge and understanding of an economic activity (4 marks); Describes the nature and spatial patterns of the global economic activity (3 marks); Outlines the nature and spatial patterns of the global economic activity (2 marks); Provides some relevant information (1 mark). Sample answer: Viticulture is an agricultural activity involving the cultivation of Vitis vinifera for the production and consumption of wine. It is a highly skilled and mechanised industry, influenced by globalisation and technological innovation in farming. This has led to viticulture now covering over 7.4 million hectares of vineyards globally, with more than 45% of wine bottles crossing international borders to both established (Japan) and new markets (India and China). Viticulture has historically been an Old World phenomenon, with Spain, France and Italy dominating global production, as wine is inherently connected to cultural norms such as the Mediterranean diet. However, the industry has expanded into New World markets, including Australia, New Zealand, Chile, Argentina and China. Today, wine is produced in over 90 countries, generating approximately 272 million hectolitres of wine annually (OIV, 2024). Wine is typically grown in the 'wine belt' between 40-50N and 30-40S of the equator in Mediterranean climates characterised by long, warm to hot summers and cool, wet winters. These conditions support optimal sugar production, as the enzymes responsible function best between 18-33C, resulting in complex and character-rich wines. This has led to the emergence of globally iconic regions, such as Shiraz from the Barossa Valley (Australia) and Riesling from Mosel (Germany)."}, {"id": "indep2025_q19a", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [41], "marks": 6, "directive": "How", "question": "Refer to your knowledge of ONE large city of 5 million people or more, outside of Australia, to answer Question 19(a). How have geographical processes shaped the large city over time?", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Clearly explains how geographical processes have shaped ONE large city of 5 million people or more, outside of Australia; demonstrates a deep understanding of rural and urban places (6 marks); Provides some explanation of the geographical processes which have shaped ONE large city (5 marks); Describes the geographic processes relating to ONE large city (4 marks); Outlines the geographic processes relating to ONE large city; shows a basic understanding of urban places (3 marks); Identifies geographic process/es relevant to ONE large city (2 marks); Provides some relevant information (1 mark). Sample answer: NYC is a large metropolitan area in the Northeast of the USA. NYC is made up of five boroughs: Manhattan, Brooklyn, Queens, the Bronx and Staten Island with a population of 8.5 million. One geographical process that has shaped NYC is globalisation. NYC underwent this process in the 1970s, where it shifted from a manufacturing-based economy to a services-based economy amplified by TNCs residing in NYC during this period. This has shaped NYC to be an economic powerhouse the way it is known as today. For example, NYC's character is as a diverse political, economic and cultural identity with a significant sphere of influence, evidenced by 6.6 million annual tourists per year, the Headquarters of 54 Fortune 500 companies, and major political institutions such as the United Nations found in the city. This process has also meant that it is now home to over 1000 MNCs and 40 000 workers. Another key process shaping NYC is the urban heat island (UHI) effect. This occurs due to the dense concentration of concrete, asphalt and built infrastructure, which absorb and retain heat, making urban areas significantly warmer than surrounding rural regions. In NYC, summer temperatures can be up to 7C higher in the city than nearby rural areas (NYC Mayor's Office of Sustainability, 2020). This effect disproportionately impacts low income and marginalised communities, where access to green spaces and cooling infrastructure is limited. This has influenced NYC's character as to address this, the city has implemented initiatives like Cool Neighbourhoods NYC, which includes planting over 1 million trees, installing green roofs and expanding cool roofing across 10 000 buildings."}, {"id": "indep2025_q19b", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [24], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Refer to your knowledge of rural and urban places to answer Question 19(b). Describe TWO influences on the size and spatial distribution of settlements.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Describes TWO influences on the size and spatial distribution of settlements; includes clear and relevant examples (4 marks); Attempts to describe TWO influences; includes some relevant examples (3 marks); Outlines TWO influences on the size and spatial distribution of settlements (2 marks); Provides some relevant information (1 mark). Sample answer: One influence on the size and spatial distribution of the world's largest cities is climate. Large cities tend to reside within the temperate climate belt, such as London or New York. This is because these conditions are the best for agriculture, the basis of economic growth for many cities. This further reflects why fewer cities in harsh climates have large populations, as the climate means that a lot more resources will be needed to sustain the needs of the population. Another influence is topography, which is best illustrated with the settlement patterns of the Amalfi Coast in southern Italy, which is characterised by its steep cliffs and ravines. The coastline features dramatic cliffs that rise sharply from the Mediterranean Sea. This steep terrain limits the available flat land for urban development. The presence of narrow valleys between the cliffs creates small pockets of land where villages can be established. This has resulted in a focus on tourism, rather than commercial and industrial development, leading to over 5 million tourists per annum and the settlement being inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1997 for its outstanding cultural landscape, combining natural beauty with human settlement and historical significance."}, {"id": "indep2025_q20a", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [17], "marks": 3, "directive": "How", "question": "Refer to your knowledge of ecosystems and global biodiversity to answer Questions 20(a)-(b). How do Indigenous Peoples contribute to the contemporary management of ecosystems?", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Explains the role of Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems (3 marks); Describes the role of Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems (2 marks); Identifies/outlines the role of Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems (1 mark). Sample answer: Indigenous Peoples in various regions of the world have maintained a relationship of stewardship with ecosystems, whereby they act as custodians of the land, responsible for protecting and ensuring sustainability for generations to come. Increasingly, governments and the organisations that manage ecosystems have acknowledged the importance of indigenous knowledge and adopted indigenous land management approaches, directly involving Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems. This can be seen in the management of the Great Barrier Reef (GBR) where Traditional Use of Marine Resources Agreements (TUMRA) and Indigenous Land and Sea Ranger Programs have been implemented. These strategies in the GBR draw upon the deep knowledge of First Nations groups within the marine park and directly involve these groups in the responses and strategies used for maintaining ecosystem functioning, biodiversity and sustainability, illustrating the role that Indigenous Peoples have in contemporary ecosystem management."}, {"id": "indep2025_q20b", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [15], "marks": 4, "directive": "Discuss", "question": "\"Over time, each generation may come to accept a more degraded environment as 'normal'.\" Discuss this statement with reference to the impact of shifting baselines on the global state of ecosystems.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Provides a discussion of the impact of shifting baselines on the global state of ecosystems; makes explicit reference to the statement (4 marks); Describes the impact of shifting baselines on the global state of ecosystems; makes implied reference to the statement (3 marks); Outlines the concept of shifting baselines in relation to the global state of ecosystems (2 marks); Makes a series of general statements about shifting baselines or the global state of ecosystems (1 mark). Sample answer: Baseline data refers to the initial set of information which acts as a point of reference for which subsequent data can be compared to, allowing for observations to be made on progress, effectiveness of strategies or the impact of changes. This data is extremely important for researchers studying the global state of ecosystems, in monitoring the health of the natural world and the impact of management on restoring biodiversity and ecological stability. The concept of shifting baselines, however, presents a significant problem to the global state of ecosystems and their future. Shifting baselines refers to the phenomenon in which society's perceptions of what is considered as 'normal' or 'healthy' in the environment changes over time - what may have been considered as degradation in an ecosystem in the past, may now be looked at as normal and accepted. This process, also referred to as Shifting Baseline Syndrome, is detrimental to the global state of ecosystems and their future sustainability, as it changes what is acceptable in regard to conservation. Additionally, it significantly impacts ecosystem management, as responses and strategies can be less effective due to baseline data being too conservative, and minimising society's efforts to address issues in the natural world."}, {"id": "indep2025_q22a", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [18], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the spatial patterns and nature of biodiversity in ONE ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Provides a clear description of the spatial pattern and nature of biodiversity in an ecosystem studied; demonstrates an extensive understanding of the ecosystem studied (4 marks); Describes the spatial pattern and/or nature of biodiversity in an ecosystem studied; demonstrates sound understanding of the ecosystem studied (3 marks); Identifies and outlines spatial patterns and/or characteristics of biodiversity in an ecosystem studied (2 marks); Makes a series of general statements about an ecosystem studied (1 mark). Sample answer: The Great Barrier Reef (GBR) is an aquatic ecosystem located off the east coast of Queensland, Australia, which covers an area of 344 400 square kilometres and spans 2300 kilometres north to south between 8-24S of the equator in the Tropic of Capricorn. The GBR is made up of over 2000 individual reefs, along with other physical features such as coral cays and islands. Additionally, it is one of the most biodiverse ecosystems on the planet, with thousands of individual species, including over 1600 species of fish and approximately 600 species of corals. Coral requires very specific conditions to survive and are a keystone species in the GBR, acting as the primary producers for energy in the system and providing a physical home to the immense number of marine species inhabiting the reef ecosystem. Changes in the environmental conditions of the GBR, particularly climate change, can have a significant impact on corals and greatly influence the dynamic equilibrium of the entire ecosystem."}, {"id": "indep2025_q22c", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [21], "marks": 10, "directive": "Analyse", "question": "Analyse the responses and strategies used for maintaining ecosystem functioning in ONE ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: Provides a detailed analysis of responses and strategies used for maintaining ecosystem functioning in an ecosystem studied; refers to relevant illustrative examples from an ecosystem studied; presents a logical and cohesive response using appropriate geographical terms and concepts (9-10 marks); Analyses responses and strategies used for maintaining ecosystem functioning; refers to illustrative examples; presents a structured response (7-8 marks); Outlines responses and strategies used for maintaining ecosystem functioning; may refer to illustrative examples (5-6 marks); Identifies responses and strategies used in ecosystems; refers to some geographical terms and/or concepts (3-4 marks); Makes a series of general statements about an ecosystem studied (1-2 marks). Sample answer: Australia's Great Barrier Reef (GBR) faces considerable threats from both natural and human-induced impacts and, thus, requires an extensive plethora of responses and strategies to maintain its functioning and sustainability. The GBR is the world's largest coral reef ecosystem; located off the Queensland coast in the Coral Sea, it stretches 2300 kilometres from 8-24S and covers an area of 344 400 square kilometres. As one of the most biodiverse ecosystems on the planet its protection for future generations is of the utmost importance. The key threats to the GBR include anthropogenic climate change and the associated coral bleaching, ocean acidification, increasing storm activity and rising sea levels, along with various other human activities (commercial fishing, shipping, mining, agriculture, pollution, etc.) and ecological disturbances such as the Crown-of-Thorns-Starfish (COTS) and tropical cyclones. The GBR became a World Heritage site in 1981 and is therefore primarily managed by the Commonwealth (Federal) Government, with the GBR Marine Park Authority (GBRMPA) acting as the main advisory body, however local governments and the Queensland State Government also play roles in the care and protection of the ecosystem. In recent years, the need to protect the GBR has been made abundantly clear, with reports conducted by the World Wildlife Fund (WWF) stating that the ecosystem may be set for total collapse, based on current projections, by 2050 - highlighting the need for effective responses and strategies that consider both intergenerational and intragenerational equity. The first example of this is zoning - introduced in 2004 by the GBRMPA, zoning maps outline areas where specific activities, such as commercial fishing, tourism developments and First Nations' use of marine resources, can occur. Significant penalties are given to those that do not abide by the zoning maps, which are made available freely online, and this strategy ensures that both current and future generations can interact with, and benefit from, the reef sustainably. Additionally, strict regulations and policies have been placed on human activities from tourism, to mining, agriculture, shipping and fishing. This includes education of tourists and the Environmental Management Charge ('Reef Tax') paid in all tourist activities, funding marine park management; large vessels requiring a 'sea pilot' to alleviate the risk of ships striking the reef; and, the Reef 2050 Water Quality Improvement Plan, aimed at addressing issues with pollutants from agriculture along the coast. A growing emphasis has also been placed on the integration of First Nations' knowledge and methods within contemporary management, which has led to the introduction of Traditional Use of Marine Resources Agreements (TUMRA), the Land and Sea Ranger Program, and the Indigenous Reef Advisory Committee (IRAC) - all of which lean on the extensive knowledge and history of First Nations people in and around the reef to improve overall management. Finally, scientific research and innovation, such as exploring the potential of super corals, artificial insemination of corals, or COTS extermination programs, are continuing to expand the repertoire of responses to changes in the functioning and sustainability of the GBR - combined with the many strategies above, the reef may yet have a future beyond 2050, and one that allows for current generations to witness its wonder and beauty, without the loss of this opportunity for the generations to come."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_01", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [6], "marks": 3, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline the nature of global tourism as a global economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n3 marks - Outlines the nature of global tourism as a global economic activity\n2 marks - Describes an aspect of the nature of global tourism\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Global tourism is a global economic activity involving the movement of people to destinations outside their usual environment for leisure, business or other purposes. It generates income and employment through accommodation, transport, food and recreation services, and ranges in scale from small heritage towns like Kiama, NSW, to large international resort destinations, making it one of the world's largest and most geographically widespread service industries."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_02", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [6], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the spatial patterns of global tourism.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks - Describes the spatial patterns of global tourism with named examples\n3 marks - Describes the spatial patterns of global tourism\n2 marks - Outlines a spatial pattern of global tourism\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Global tourism flows are highly uneven, concentrated around coastal and natural-attraction destinations, historic/cultural cities, and alpine or resort regions. Flows are strongest from wealthy, developed source countries towards both nearby and distant destinations, often clustering around transport hubs (airports, highways). In NSW, this pattern is visible at a smaller scale: coastal towns such as Kiama attract day-trippers and short-stay visitors from Sydney and Wollongong due to their proximity and coastal attractions (the Blowhole, beaches), while alpine destinations such as Thredbo attract longer-stay, seasonal visitor flows tied to the snow season and, increasingly, summer activities."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_03", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [7], "marks": 3, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline how technological factors have influenced global tourism, with reference to Kiama, NSW.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n3 marks - Outlines how technological factors have influenced global tourism, with reference to Kiama\n2 marks - Describes a technological factor affecting global tourism\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Technological factors have influenced tourism at Kiama through Kiama Council's exploration of emerging technologies such as AI for customer service and planning, alongside modern transport and communication infrastructure that make the town easier for visitors to reach and navigate. Technology is also applied to water conservation and waste management, helping the town manage the environmental pressure that a high volume of day-trip and short-stay tourists places on local infrastructure."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_04", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [7], "marks": 5, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how political and organisational factors have affected the spatial patterns of global tourism, with reference to Kiama, NSW.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5 marks - Explains how political and organisational factors have affected the spatial patterns of global tourism, with reference to Kiama\n4 marks - Explains ONE of political or organisational factors, with some reference to the other\n3 marks - Describes political and/or organisational factors affecting tourism\n2 marks - Outlines a factor affecting tourism\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Kiama is governed by Kiama Municipal Council, an organisational factor that directly shapes where and how tourism development occurs through local planning and development approvals, and through its stated commitment to sustainable development and managing growth while supporting local businesses. Politically, Kiama sits within the NSW Legislative Assembly electoral district of Kiama, currently held by an independent member; local and state government policies on development and environmental conservation significantly influence which areas are protected from development (concentrating tourism around existing attractions like the Blowhole and heritage Queen Street-style precincts) and which are opened up for new visitor infrastructure, directly shaping the spatial pattern of where tourism activity is permitted to grow."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_05", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [7], "marks": 6, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how biophysical and economic factors have influenced the nature of global tourism, with reference to Kiama, NSW.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks - Explains how biophysical and economic factors have influenced the nature of global tourism, with well-integrated reference to Kiama\n3-4 marks - Explains ONE of biophysical or economic factors, or describes both\n1-2 marks - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Biophysical factors are central to Kiama's tourism identity: the famous Kiama Blowhole and rugged coastal cliffs are the town's signature natural attraction, while its temperate climate (mild winters, warm summers) and diverse vegetation, including rainforest, eucalypt forest and coastal wetlands, support both scenic appeal and nature-based activities. Some of this vegetation supports threatened or endangered species, which shapes the nature of tourism towards low-impact, nature-viewing activity rather than intensive development. Economically, tourism operates alongside agriculture (dairy farming) and horticulture as core industries, and the Blowhole, beaches and heritage buildings draw visitors year-round, supporting a service economy of cafes, accommodation and retail. Together, these factors have produced a tourism industry centred on short, nature- and heritage-based visits rather than large-scale resort tourism."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_07", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [9], "marks": 3, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe ONE environmental impact of global tourism, with reference to Kiama, NSW.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n3 marks - Describes an environmental impact of global tourism, with reference to Kiama\n2 marks - Outlines an environmental impact of global tourism\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: A key environmental impact of tourism at Kiama is pressure on sensitive coastal ecosystems, including rainforest, eucalypt forest and coastal wetland vegetation that supports some threatened or endangered species. Rising visitor numbers around attractions like the Blowhole increase foot traffic, litter and demand on water and waste infrastructure, which is why Kiama Council has had to invest in technology for water conservation and waste management to reduce tourism's footprint on these environments."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_08", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [9], "marks": 6, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain the social impact and economic impact of global tourism, with reference to a place you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks - Explains both a social impact and an economic impact of global tourism, with well-integrated reference to a place studied\n3-4 marks - Explains ONE impact, or describes both\n1-2 marks - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: At Kiama, tourism has a significant economic impact: alongside dairy farming and horticulture, it is a core driver of the local economy, supporting cafes, accommodation, retail and heritage-building based businesses that rely on year-round visitor spending, and Kiama Council actively invests in infrastructure to sustain this. Socially, tourism brings both benefits and costs to the community: it supports local employment and community identity around the town's heritage and natural attractions, but high visitor volumes, especially on weekends, can strain local infrastructure and create congestion that affects residents' quality of life, which is part of why the council frames its approach around 'managing growth while supporting local businesses' rather than simply maximising visitor numbers."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_09", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [10], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe TWO strategies used to improve the sustainability of global tourism, with reference to Thredbo Alpine Resort.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks - Describes TWO strategies used to improve the sustainability of tourism at Thredbo\n3 marks - Describes ONE strategy, or outlines TWO\n2 marks - Outlines ONE strategy\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: One strategy at Thredbo is operating on 100% renewable energy and using biodegradable packaging to replace single-use plastics, directly reducing the resort's carbon footprint and waste stream. A second strategy is diversifying income beyond winter skiing into summer activities such as mountain biking, hiking and fishing, which spreads visitor demand (and therefore environmental and infrastructure pressure) more evenly across the year rather than concentrating it in a short peak season, while also making the resort less economically vulnerable to a poor snow season."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_10", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [10], "marks": 6, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain TWO factors that have contributed to the success of a sustainability strategy in global tourism, with reference to Thredbo Alpine Resort.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks - Explains TWO factors contributing to the success of a named sustainability strategy at Thredbo\n3-4 marks - Explains ONE factor, or describes TWO\n1-2 marks - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Thredbo's environmental sustainability strategy has been supported by strong organisational commitment, shown through achieving Gold EarthCheck certification and partnering with Greenfleet to offset over 12,450 tonnes of carbon emissions by planting around 45,000 native trees - external accreditation and partnership that both validate the strategy and create ongoing accountability. A second contributing factor is technological investment, such as SNOWsat technology for more efficient snow grooming, which saves energy and water while maintaining the ski product visitors expect; because this technology delivers a cost saving alongside an environmental one, it gives the resort a strong ongoing incentive to maintain and extend the strategy rather than treating it as a one-off initiative."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_11", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [11], "marks": 4, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline ONE strategy for achieving sustainability in global tourism, with reference to Thredbo Alpine Resort.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks - Outlines a named strategy for achieving sustainability in tourism, with reference to Thredbo\n3 marks - Describes an aspect of the strategy\n2 marks - Identifies the strategy\n1 mark - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer: Thredbo Alpine Resort's environmental sustainability strategy centres on decarbonising its operations: it runs on 100% renewable energy, has partnered with Greenfleet to offset over 12,450 tonnes of carbon emissions through planting approximately 45,000 native trees, employs electric vehicles in resort operations, and holds Gold EarthCheck certification. The strategy also extends to habitat restoration within Kosciuszko National Park and closed-loop organic recycling that turns waste into compost, reflecting a broad approach that targets emissions, waste and biodiversity together rather than any single issue in isolation."}, {"id": "trial2026-w1", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [18, 19], "marks": 2, "directive": "Identify", "question": "Identify ONE example of primary data and ONE example of secondary information that a geography student could collect when investigating the ecological health of an ecosystem.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 2 marks \u2013 Identifies ONE example of primary data and ONE example of secondary information that a geography student could collect when investigating the ecological health of an ecosystem. 1 mark \u2013 Identifies ONE relevant example of primary data OR secondary information.\n\nSample answer: Primary data: % of kelp measured by quadrat across a transect. Secondary information: Satellite photography of a kelp forest showing change over time."}, {"id": "trial2026-w2", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [15, 18], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline ONE way a geography student might use remote sensing in an ecosystem investigation.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 2 marks \u2013 Outlines ONE way a geography student could use remote sensing in an ecosystem investigation. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information.\n\nSample answer: One way a student could use remote sensing is by analysing satellite imagery of an area over time to track changes in vegetation or canopy cover. This allows the student to identify areas of degradation, regrowth or clearing."}, {"id": "trial2026-w3", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [13], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline ONE resource that ecosystems provide for humans.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 2 marks \u2013 Outlines ONE resource that ecosystems provide for humans. 1 mark \u2013 Identifies a resource that ecosystems provide for humans.\n\nSample answer: Ecosystems provide provisioning resources such as freshwater. Forested catchments and wetlands filter and store water, ensuring a reliable supply for drinking, agriculture and industry. They also help maintain water quality by removing pollutants and reducing sediment runoff, supporting human wellbeing."}, {"id": "trial2026-w4", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [12, 18], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the role of ONE plant or animal species that is important to the functioning of an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 4 marks \u2013 Describes the role of ONE plant or animal species that is important to the functioning of an ecosystem. 3 marks \u2013 Outlines the role of ONE plant or animal species that is important to the functioning of an ecosystem. 2 marks \u2013 Outlines one or more aspects of ONE plant or animal species. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information about plants or animals.\n\nSample answer: Dipterocarp trees are a keystone species in the Borneo rainforest, forming the emergent canopy and providing food, shelter and breeding habitats for a wide range of animals, including orangutans, hornbills and insects. Their irregular mass fruiting events influence population dynamics across the ecosystem by supplying large quantities of food. Extensive root systems help stabilise soils and support nutrient cycling in nutrient-poor conditions. As a result, the loss of dipterocarps through logging can reduce biodiversity, disrupt ecosystem processes and weaken overall ecosystem functioning."}, {"id": "trial2026-w5", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [17], "marks": 5, "directive": "Discuss", "question": "Discuss the role played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 5 marks \u2013 Discusses clearly and thoroughly the role played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems. 4 marks \u2013 Provides some discussion of the role played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems. 3 marks \u2013 Describes the role played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems. 2 marks \u2013 Outlines ONE role played by Indigenous Peoples in the contemporary management of ecosystems. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information.\n\nSample answer: Indigenous Peoples contribute significantly to ecosystem management through Traditional Ecological Knowledge (TEK), developed through thousands of years of environmental stewardship. In Australia, Indigenous Ranger programs combine TEK with Western scientific methods to manage fire regimes, control invasive species and restore degraded environments. The regeneration of the Jewell Wetlands demonstrates how Indigenous knowledge and controlled ecological burns can support ecosystem recovery. However, the success of these approaches depends on genuine collaboration with Indigenous communities rather than tokenistic consultation. Overall, Indigenous involvement improves ecological resilience, supports cultural continuity and strengthens the long-term sustainability of ecosystem management."}, {"id": "trial2026-w6", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [34], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Answer with reference to ONE large city outside Australia that you have studied. Outline the location of the place.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 2 marks \u2013 Outlines the location of ONE large city outside of Australia. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information.\n\nSample answer: Shenzhen is in Guangdong Province, southern China, in the Pearl River Delta. It borders Hong Kong to the south and Guangzhou to the north."}, {"id": "trial2026-w7", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [35], "marks": 3, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Answer with reference to ONE large city outside Australia that you have studied. Explain ONE geographical process that has shaped the identity of the place.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 3 marks \u2013 Explains ONE geographical process that has shaped the identity of the place. 2 marks \u2013 Outlines ONE geographical process that has shaped the identity of the place. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information.\n\nSample answer: Economic transformation has shaped Shenzhen\u2019s identity since it became China\u2019s first Special Economic Zone in 1980. The opening of trade and investment attracted foreign businesses and rapid industrialisation. As a result, Shenzhen grew from a small fishing town into a global technology centre, home to companies such as Huawei, Tencent and DJI, earning the nickname \u201cChina\u2019s Silicon Valley\u201d."}, {"id": "trial2026-w8", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [27, 28], "marks": 6, "directive": "Assess", "question": "Assess the strategies that help maintain a small ecological footprint and a high level of wellbeing in ONE settlement you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 6 marks \u2013 Assesses thoroughly the strategies that help maintain a small ecological footprint AND a high level of wellbeing in ONE settlement. 5 marks \u2013 Assesses the strategies that help maintain a small ecological footprint AND a high level of wellbeing in ONE settlement. 4 marks \u2013 Assesses the strategies that help maintain a small ecological footprint OR a high level of wellbeing in ONE settlement. 2\u20133 marks \u2013 Outlines strategies that help maintain a small ecological footprint OR a high level of wellbeing in ONE settlement. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information to question.\n\nSample answer: Oslo has implemented highly successful strategies to maintain a small ecological footprint while supporting a high level of wellbeing. The city relies heavily on renewable hydroelectric power and has strongly encouraged electric vehicle adoption through government incentives, significantly reducing transport emissions. Sustainable transport initiatives, including electric ferries, hydrogen-electric buses and extensive cycling infrastructure, have further reduced carbon output and dependence on private cars. These strategies have been highly effective in improving wellbeing by reducing air pollution, supporting public health and providing reliable transport options. As a result, Oslo is widely recognised as one of the world\u2019s most sustainable and liveable cities. However, their overall success is somewhat limited by high living costs, meaning benefits are not accessed equally by all residents."}, {"id": "trial2026-w9", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [27], "marks": 2, "directive": "Define", "question": "Define the term \u2018ecological footprint\u2019.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 2 marks \u2013 Defines the term \u2018ecological footprint\u2019. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information.\n\nSample answer: Ecological footprint is a measure, expressed in global hectares (gha), of the demand human activity places on the Earth\u2019s ecosystems \u2014 the amount of land and water required to produce the resources consumed and absorb the waste generated by a population."}, {"id": "trial2026-w10", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [8], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Answer with reference to a global economic activity you have studied. Outline ONE future trend in the global economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 2 marks \u2013 Outlines ONE future trend in a global economic activity. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information about a global economic activity.\n\nSample answer: One future trend in tourism is growth in the outbound market from developing countries, as rising incomes and an expanding middle class \u2014 particularly in Asia \u2014 reshape global travel patterns, with more residents of these nations becoming international tourists rather than only receiving them."}, {"id": "trial2026-w11", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [9, 10], "marks": 6, "directive": "Discuss", "question": "Answer with reference to a global economic activity you have studied. Discuss TWO strategies businesses or governments can use to manage the environmental sustainability of the economic activity.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 6 marks \u2013 Clearly discusses TWO strategies businesses or governments can use for environmental sustainability within the global economic activity. 5 marks \u2013 Discusses TWO strategies businesses or governments can use for environmental sustainability within the global economic activity. 4 marks \u2013 Describes TWO strategies for environmental sustainability within the global economic activity. 2\u20133 marks \u2013 Outlines ONE or more strategies for sustainability within the global economic activity. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information to question.\n\nSample answer: Two strategies used to improve the environmental sustainability of global tourism are visitor quotas and renewable energy transition, both demonstrated by Thredbo Alpine Resort. Visitor quotas are effective because they limit tourist numbers in environmentally sensitive areas, reducing erosion, habitat damage and waste generation. However, critics argue that quotas can restrict visitor access and reduce income for tourism businesses that depend on high visitor numbers. Renewable energy transition involves tourism operators using cleaner energy sources to reduce greenhouse gas emissions. Thredbo operates on 100% renewable energy and uses technologies that reduce water and energy consumption. This strategy is highly effective in lowering the industry's environmental impact; however, the high installation costs can make adoption difficult for smaller operators. Overall, both strategies make a significant contribution to sustainable tourism."}, {"id": "trial2026-w12", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [16], "marks": 6, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "Evaluate ONE successful ecosystem conservation program for its sustainability.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 6 marks \u2013 Evaluates with criteria ONE successful ecosystem conservation program for its sustainability. 4\u20135 marks \u2013 Assesses ONE successful ecosystem conservation program for its sustainability. 3 marks \u2013 Describes ONE ecosystem conservation program. 2 marks \u2013 Outlines ONE ecosystem conservation program. 1 mark \u2013 Makes general statements related to ecosystem conservation.\n\nSample answer: The tiger conservation program in Thailand\u2019s Eastern and Western Forest Complexes is a successful ecosystem conservation program when evaluated against ecological effectiveness, community engagement and long-term viability. Ecologically, it has been highly effective, with trail cameras, SMART patrolling and surveillance technology reducing poaching and supporting tiger population recovery, evidenced by the return of tigers and the observation of new cubs. The program has also engaged local communities by supporting livelihoods and involving residents in conservation activities, increasing long-term support for protection efforts. Its sustainability is further strengthened through ongoing monitoring, international cooperation and education campaigns that address illegal wildlife trade. Overall, the program demonstrates strong sustainability because it combines technology, enforcement and community participation. However, its long-term success remains dependent on continued funding and effective control of poaching and illegal wildlife markets."}, {"id": "trial2026-w13", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [18], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Understanding ecosystem spatial patterns, the value of natural places and approaches to research is important when managing ecosystems.\n\n(a) Outline the spatial pattern of ONE ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 2 marks \u2013 Outlines the spatial pattern of ONE ecosystem. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information.\n\nSample answer: The Great Southern Reef is a temperate kelp forest ecosystem extending approximately 8,000 km along the continental shelf of southern Australia, from northern New South Wales to Kalbarri in Western Australia. It covers around 71,000 km\u00b2 at depths of 30\u201360 metres in the sub-tidal zone, dominated by Golden Kelp (Ecklonia radiata)."}, {"id": "trial2026-w14", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [13], "marks": 6, "directive": "Analyse", "question": "Understanding ecosystem spatial patterns, the value of natural places and approaches to research is important when managing ecosystems.\n\n(b) Analyse the value of the ecosystem you outlined in part (a) (i.e. ONE ecosystem you have studied).", "markingGuideline": "Criteria: 6 marks \u2013 Identifies components of value in an ecosystem and draws out and relates implications for each component thoroughly. 5 marks \u2013 Identifies components of value in an ecosystem and draws out and relates some implications. 3\u20134 marks \u2013 Describes the value of an ecosystem. 2 marks \u2013 Outlines the value of an ecosystem. 1 mark \u2013 Provides some relevant information.\n\nSample answer: The Great Southern Reef provides important supporting, provisioning, regulating and cultural values. As a supporting service, kelp forests drive primary productivity and nutrient cycling, forming the foundation of marine food webs. This enables provisioning services, as valuable fisheries such as abalone, rock lobster and finfish depend on healthy kelp habitats for their survival. The reef also delivers regulating value by storing carbon, reducing wave energy and improving water quality, helping protect coastal environments. Culturally, it supports tourism, recreation and Indigenous connections to Country, contributing to social wellbeing and local economies. These values are closely linked: if kelp forests decline, fish stocks, coastal protection and recreational opportunities are also reduced. Therefore, protecting the reef\u2019s supporting functions is essential to maintaining its broader ecological, economic and cultural value."}, {"id": "trial2026-w15", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [23], "marks": 12, "directive": "Assess", "question": "Understanding ecosystem spatial patterns, the value of natural places and approaches to research is important when managing ecosystems.\n\n(c) Assess the role of contemporary research and innovation in the sustainable management of the ecosystem you outlined in part (a) (i.e. ONE ecosystem you have studied).", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (extended-response marking bands):\n11\u201312 marks \u2013 Demonstrates comprehensive knowledge and understanding of the role of contemporary research and innovation in the sustainable management of an ecosystem; makes extensive judgement about the value, quality, outcomes, results or size of the role; integrates relevant case studies, illustrative examples and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate; presents a logical and cohesive response using appropriate geographical information, terms and concepts.\n8\u201310 marks \u2013 Demonstrates well-developed knowledge and understanding of the role of contemporary research and innovation in the sustainable management of an ecosystem; makes some judgement about the value, quality, outcomes, results or size of the role; refers to relevant case studies, illustrative examples and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate; presents a logical response using appropriate geographical information and terms.\n5\u20137 marks \u2013 Demonstrates a sound understanding of the role of contemporary research and innovation in the sustainable management of an ecosystem; describes contemporary research and innovation; refers to case studies, illustrative examples and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate; presents a structured response using appropriate geographical information.\n3\u20134 marks \u2013 Demonstrates some basic knowledge and understanding of contemporary research and innovation in the sustainable management of an ecosystem; may refer to case studies, illustrative examples and/or the Stimulus Booklet; uses some geographical information.\n1\u20132 marks \u2013 Demonstrates elementary understanding of contemporary research and innovation in sustainable management.\n\nAnswers could include (Great Southern Reef kelp forest example):\n- Active kelp restoration (Queenscliff Marine Science Centre): Over six million Golden Kelp spores cultivated on twine and gravel for deployment in Port Phillip Bay, an area degraded by purple urchin overgrazing (\u201curchin barrens\u201d); represents a shift from passive protection to active, research-driven habitat rebuilding.\n- Genetic resilience (Golden Kelp seed bank): Victoria\u2019s first Golden Kelp seed bank preserves genetic diversity sourced from multiple locations across Port Phillip Bay; safeguards the genetic resilience needed for kelp to adapt to future environmental stress as climate change continues to warm GSR waters.\n- Green Gravel technique: Juvenile kelp seeded onto small gravel stones under controlled conditions, then deployed at scale into degraded reef areas; scalable and community-accessible, allowing broader participation in restoration; enables scientists to select thermally tolerant genotypes, \u201cfuture-proofing\u201d reefs against further warming and addressing the root cause of kelp decline, not just its symptoms.\n- Marine Protected Areas (MPAs): Supported by ongoing research and monitoring, MPAs provide a complementary management layer by limiting harmful activities such as overfishing and habitat destruction; effectiveness depends on adequate enforcement and community compliance.\n- Judgement may include: Research and innovation have been highly effective in advancing GSR management, moving conservation beyond protection alone towards active, science-led restoration that targets the specific mechanisms of kelp decline \u2014 overgrazing, genetic vulnerability and thermal stress. However, these innovations remain localised and resource-intensive; techniques such as Green Gravel and spore cultivation currently operate at a small scale relative to the reef\u2019s 71,000 km\u00b2 extent. Research and innovation are indispensable to the GSR\u2019s future but cannot alone offset climate change impacts \u2014 they must be paired with broader emissions reduction and MPA enforcement to achieve reef-wide resilience."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-1", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 6, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how biophysical interactions contribute to the functioning of an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks \u2013 explains multiple, named biophysical interactions (e.g. energy flows, nutrient cycling, food webs, climate\u2013vegetation\u2013soil relationships) using specific detail from a studied ecosystem, and links these clearly to how the ecosystem functions.\n3-4 marks \u2013 describes biophysical interactions with some specific detail but limited explanation of functioning.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statements about ecosystems with little specific detail.\n\nSample approach: name the ecosystem, then explain at least two linked interactions \u2014 e.g. in a coral reef, symbiosis between coral polyps and zooxanthellae drives primary productivity, which supports a food web from herbivorous fish to apex predators, while nutrient cycling (e.g. via parrotfish grazing and bioerosion) recycles nutrients that would otherwise be scarce in nutrient-poor tropical waters."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-2", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Using an example, describe how nutrient cycling affects the functioning of an ecosystem.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 describes nutrient cycling with specific, named detail (e.g. a named nutrient pathway, storage, and named ecosystem) and links it to ecosystem functioning.\n2-3 marks \u2013 describes nutrient cycling in general terms or with a limited example.\n1 mark \u2013 identifies that nutrient cycling occurs, with little detail.\n\nSample approach: e.g. in a mangrove ecosystem, leaf litter decomposes and returns nitrogen and phosphorus to sediments, which are taken up by mangrove roots and by algae that support fish nurseries \u2014 disruption to this cycle (e.g. clearing) reduces productivity throughout the food web."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-3", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 6, "directive": "Discuss", "question": "Discuss how ONE ecosystem you have studied is both vulnerable and resilient to natural and human-induced change.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks \u2013 discusses both vulnerability (susceptibility to damage) AND resilience (capacity to recover) with specific, named evidence for the studied ecosystem.\n3-4 marks \u2013 discusses one of vulnerability or resilience with specific detail, or both in general terms.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statements only.\n\nSample approach: e.g. the Great Barrier Reef is vulnerable to ocean warming (coral bleaching events) and crown-of-thorns starfish outbreaks, yet shows resilience through coral recruitment after disturbance and genetic diversity across the reef system that allows some coral populations to recover faster than others."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-4", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 2, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline how ONE example of human-induced stress has affected the functioning of an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n2 marks \u2013 outlines a named human-induced stress and its effect on functioning.\n1 mark \u2013 identifies a stress with limited or no explanation of effect.\n\nSample approach: e.g. urban stormwater runoff into a wetland introduces excess nutrients, triggering algal blooms that reduce oxygen levels and disrupt the wetland's food web."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-5", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how the removal of ONE species from a food web could affect the diversity and functioning of an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 explains how removing a named species disrupts named trophic relationships, with a clear consequence for diversity and functioning.\n2-3 marks \u2013 describes an effect with some specific detail.\n1 mark \u2013 general statement only.\n\nSample approach: e.g. removing sea otters (a keystone predator) from a kelp forest allows sea urchin populations to grow unchecked, leading to overgrazing of kelp and the formation of urchin barrens, which collapses habitat for many other species and reduces biodiversity."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-6", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 2, "directive": "Identify", "question": "Identify what is meant by 'genetic diversity', and outline why it is a value used to justify protecting ecosystems.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n2 marks \u2013 correctly identifies genetic diversity (variation within a species) AND outlines its value for protection (e.g. resilience to disease/climate change, future scientific/medical use).\n1 mark \u2013 addresses only the definition or only the value.\n\nSample approach: genetic diversity is the variety of genes within a species' population. It is valued because populations with greater genetic diversity are more likely to include individuals able to survive new threats (disease, changing conditions), helping the species and ecosystem persist."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-7", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 6, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "Evaluate the importance of ecosystem management and protection, using at least TWO values (e.g. utility, intrinsic, heritage, or genetic diversity value).", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks \u2013 evaluates at least two named values with specific supporting detail and reaches a judgement about their relative or combined importance.\n3-4 marks \u2013 explains two values with some specific detail but limited judgement.\n1-2 marks \u2013 identifies values with little explanation.\n\nSample approach: utility value (e.g. a reef's role in coastal protection and fisheries) can be weighed against intrinsic value (a species' right to exist regardless of human use) \u2014 argue that while utility value is easier to quantify and often drives funding decisions, intrinsic and heritage values are essential for protecting ecosystems that have no obvious economic use but are ecologically or culturally significant."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-8", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe how ONE traditional and ONE contemporary management strategy have each been used in an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 describes one named traditional strategy AND one named contemporary strategy with specific detail.\n2-3 marks \u2013 describes one strategy in detail, or both in general terms.\n1 mark \u2013 identifies a strategy with little detail.\n\nSample approach: traditional \u2014 Indigenous cultural burning practices used to manage fuel loads and promote biodiversity in a savanna ecosystem; contemporary \u2014 satellite-monitored Marine Protected Area zoning that restricts fishing in a reef ecosystem based on real-time data."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-9", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 8, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "Evaluate the effectiveness of ONE traditional and ONE contemporary management strategy used in an ecosystem at risk you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n7-8 marks \u2013 evaluates both strategies against explicit criteria (e.g. scale, cost, measurable outcomes, community involvement), using specific named evidence, and reaches a substantiated overall judgement.\n5-6 marks \u2013 describes both strategies with some evaluation but a less sustained judgement.\n3-4 marks \u2013 describes strategies with limited evaluation.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statements only.\n\nSample approach: compare a traditional practice (e.g. cultural burning) against a contemporary strategy (e.g. a Marine Protected Area or reef-restoration project) using measurable outcomes (area protected, species recovery data, cost, community engagement), concluding which is more effective and why, while acknowledging limitations of each."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-10", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the spatial patterns and dimensions (location, altitude, latitude, size, shape and continuity) of an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 describes at least three of the named dimensions with specific detail for the studied ecosystem.\n2-3 marks \u2013 describes one or two dimensions with specific detail.\n1 mark \u2013 general statement about the ecosystem's location only.\n\nSample answer: e.g. the Great Southern Reef stretches along Australia's temperate southern coastline (latitude range roughly 27\u00b0S to 43\u00b0S), extending from shallow subtidal zones down to around 40m depth (altitude/depth dimension), is continuous along thousands of kilometres of coast but varies in width and continuity depending on suitable rocky substrate for kelp attachment."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-11", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe TWO negative human impacts on the functioning of an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 describes two distinct, named human impacts with specific detail and a clear link to ecosystem functioning.\n2-3 marks \u2013 describes one impact in detail, or two in general terms.\n1 mark \u2013 identifies an impact with little detail.\n\nSample approach: e.g. sewage-outfall discharge historically increased nutrient loads and reduced water clarity, smothering kelp with algal growth; and coastal development has removed nearshore vegetation buffers, increasing sediment runoff that further reduces light available for kelp photosynthesis."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-12", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how ONE natural change and ONE human-induced change have each affected an ecosystem you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 explains one named natural change AND one named human-induced change, each with a clear effect on the ecosystem.\n2-3 marks \u2013 explains one change in detail, or both in general terms.\n1 mark \u2013 identifies a change with little explanation.\n\nSample approach: natural \u2014 a marine heatwave causing coral bleaching and short-term loss of live coral cover; human-induced \u2014 coastal armouring (seawalls) altering natural sediment movement and removing intertidal habitat over time."}, {"id": "adapt2-t3-1", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the nature, character and spatial distribution of ONE urban place you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 describes nature, character AND spatial distribution with specific, named detail.\n2-3 marks \u2013 describes two of the three elements with some specific detail.\n1 mark \u2013 general statement only.\n\nSample approach: e.g. Barangaroo is a high-density, mixed-use waterfront precinct on the western edge of the Sydney CBD, characterised by commercial towers, public parkland and residential apartments, reflecting Sydney's broader pattern of harbourside urban renewal and vertical growth close to the city centre."}, {"id": "adapt2-t3-2", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Account for", "question": "Account for the rapid growth of ONE urban place in the developing world that you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 accounts for growth using at least two specific, named factors (e.g. rural-urban migration, natural increase, economic opportunity, foreign investment) with supporting detail.\n2-3 marks \u2013 describes growth with one factor in detail or several in general terms.\n1 mark \u2013 general statement about growth only.\n\nSample approach: rapid growth driven by rural-urban migration in search of employment, high rates of natural increase among a young population, and foreign direct investment in manufacturing/services concentrating jobs in the urban centre."}, {"id": "adapt2-t3-3", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 6, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "Describe ONE challenge of living in an urban place in the developing world, then evaluate the effectiveness of ONE response to this challenge.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks \u2013 describes a named, specific challenge (e.g. informal settlements, traffic congestion, service provision) AND evaluates a named response using evidence, reaching a judgement about its effectiveness.\n3-4 marks \u2013 describes the challenge and response with some specific detail but limited evaluation.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statements only.\n\nSample approach: e.g. the challenge of informal settlements lacking sanitation infrastructure, evaluated against a slum-upgrading program that provided piped water and legal land tenure \u2014 judge its effectiveness using data on service coverage achieved and limitations such as funding or scale relative to total need."}, {"id": "adapt2-t3-4", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 5, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain why ONE urban place is an important centre of economic and/or cultural influence within global networks.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5 marks \u2013 explains specific, named reasons (e.g. headquarters of multinational firms, financial markets, media/cultural institutions, transport/communication hubs) with clear links to global influence.\n3-4 marks \u2013 explains with some specific detail.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statement only.\n\nSample approach: e.g. Singapore is economically influential as a global financial and shipping hub hosting regional headquarters of major multinational corporations, and culturally influential through its role as a media and education centre connecting Southeast Asia to global networks."}, {"id": "adapt2-t3-5", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 5, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain the relationship of dominance and dependence between ONE major urban place and the smaller settlements around it.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5 marks \u2013 explains specific, named ways the major urban place dominates (e.g. employment, services, transport links) AND how smaller settlements depend on it, with supporting detail.\n3-4 marks \u2013 explains the relationship with some specific detail.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statement only.\n\nSample approach: e.g. Sydney dominates surrounding regional towns as the primary source of specialist healthcare, higher education and corporate employment, while regional centres depend on Sydney for these services but also supply Sydney with food, resources and commuting labour, creating a two-way but unequal relationship."}, {"id": "adapt2-t3-6", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 3, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Outline how the changing role of regional service centres has affected small towns near them.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n3 marks \u2013 outlines a specific, named effect (e.g. loss of local shops/services, population decline, ageing population) with a clear link to the changing role of regional centres.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statement about decline with little explanation.\n\nSample approach: as regional centres consolidate services (larger supermarkets, hospitals, government offices), small nearby towns lose local businesses that can't compete, leading to population decline and an ageing remaining population as younger residents move to the regional centre for work and services."}, {"id": "adapt2-t3-7", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 3, "directive": "Outline", "question": "Identify and outline ONE urban process (dynamic) of change operating in a rural town or suburb you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n3 marks \u2013 identifies a named process (e.g. gentrification, exurbanisation, counter-urbanisation, urban consolidation) and outlines how it is operating with specific detail.\n1-2 marks \u2013 identifies the process with little or no explanation.\n\nSample approach: gentrification in an inner-suburb \u2014 older, lower-cost housing renovated and sold at higher prices, attracting higher-income residents and gradually displacing lower-income long-term residents, changing the suburb's demographic and commercial character."}, {"id": "adapt2-t3-8", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Compare", "question": "Compare TWO urban processes (dynamics) of change operating within a large city you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 compares two named processes (e.g. gentrification and urban consolidation) with specific similarities and/or differences.\n2-3 marks \u2013 describes two processes with limited comparison.\n1 mark \u2013 identifies processes with little detail.\n\nSample approach: gentrification concentrates in specific well-located inner suburbs and displaces lower-income residents, while urban consolidation (increased density via apartments) is more widely dispersed across middle-ring suburbs and adds housing supply rather than displacing existing residents."}, {"id": "adapt2-t3-9", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 3, "directive": "Describe", "question": "Describe the spatial patterns of advantage and disadvantage within a large city you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n3 marks \u2013 describes a specific spatial pattern (e.g. concentration of disadvantage in outer suburbs with poor transport access, advantage concentrated near the CBD/harbour) with named locations or features.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statement about inequality with little spatial detail.\n\nSample approach: e.g. in Sydney, advantage is concentrated in inner and harbourside suburbs with high land values and good access to jobs/services, while disadvantage is more common in outer south-western and western suburbs, associated with longer commutes and less access to services."}, {"id": "adapt2-t3-10", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 4, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how an urban process (dynamic) of change has affected the culture of place within a suburb or city you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n4 marks \u2013 explains a named process's effect on specific culture-of-place elements (e.g. streetscape, local businesses, community identity, public art) with supporting detail.\n2-3 marks \u2013 explains with some specific detail.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statement only.\n\nSample approach: e.g. gentrification in an inner-city suburb has changed its culture of place through renovated heritage terraces, a shift from independent stores to boutique cafes, and the loss of some long-standing community identity as long-term residents are priced out."}, {"id": "adapt2-t3-11", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 6, "directive": "Analyse", "question": "Analyse the impact of urban processes (dynamics) on the ecological sustainability of a large city you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks \u2013 analyses how at least two named processes affect ecological sustainability (e.g. energy use, green space, transport emissions, waste) with specific supporting evidence.\n3-4 marks \u2013 describes impacts with some analysis.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statement only.\n\nSample approach: urban consolidation (increased density) can reduce per-capita car dependency and infrastructure costs, improving sustainability, while urban sprawl associated with exurbanisation increases car dependency, loss of green space and stormwater runoff, reducing sustainability \u2014 weigh both to reach an overall judgement for the city studied."}, {"id": "adapt2-t3-12", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 6, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain how population growth and associated urban expansion affect the provision of social services and infrastructure in a rural or urban place you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n5-6 marks \u2013 explains specific, named effects on services AND infrastructure (e.g. schools, healthcare, roads, utilities), with clear links to population growth/expansion.\n3-4 marks \u2013 explains one of services or infrastructure with specific detail.\n1-2 marks \u2013 general statement only.\n\nSample approach: e.g. rapid population growth in an outer-suburban growth corridor has outpaced school and healthcare capacity, leading to overcrowded classrooms and long specialist wait times, while road and public transport infrastructure has lagged behind housing development, increasing commute times and congestion."}];
window.examEssayData = [{"id": "hsc2025_q23", "source": "2025 HSC", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [12], "marks": 20, "directive": "Explain", "question": "Explain the nature and complexity of ecosystem functioning.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (Question 23, band marking):\n17-20 marks - Demonstrates comprehensive knowledge and understanding of the nature and complexity of ecosystem functioning; Provides a detailed explanation of the nature and complexity of ecosystem functioning; Integrates relevant examples, geographical information and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate to support the response; Presents a sustained, logical and cohesive response using appropriate geographical ideas, terms and concepts\n13-16 marks - Demonstrates well-developed knowledge and understanding of the nature and complexity of ecosystem functioning; Provides an explanation of the nature and complexity of ecosystem functioning; Refers to relevant examples, geographical information and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate to support the response; Presents a logical response using appropriate geographical terminology and concepts\n9-12 marks - Demonstrates sound knowledge and understanding of the nature and/or complexity of ecosystem functioning; Describes the nature and/or complexity of ecosystem functioning; Refers to examples, geographical information and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate; Presents a structured response using appropriate geographical concepts\n5-8 marks - Demonstrates some knowledge and understanding of ecosystem functioning; May refer to examples, geographical information and/or the Stimulus Booklet; Uses some geographical terminology\n1-4 marks - Demonstrates a basic understanding of ecosystem functioning\n\nAnswers could include:\nNature of ecosystem functioning may include: abiotic and biotic interactions; spatial patterns; energy flows and nutrient cycles; dynamic equilibrium and feedback loops; relationships between natural systems.\nAspects of ecosystem complexity may include: adaptation and change such as succession; biodiversity and interdependence; differing combinations of natural systems/spheres resulting in unique and/or fragile diversity; fluctuations in functioning resulting in altered or different vulnerability and resilience."}, {"id": "sample_22", "source": "Sample Exam", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [22], "marks": 20, "directive": "Account for", "question": "Account for the differences in the management of ONE type of ecosystem at TWO different locations.", "markingGuideline": "Official marking guideline (top band, 17-20 marks): Demonstrates comprehensive knowledge and understanding of the differences in the management of ONE type of ecosystem at TWO different locations; provides highly-developed reasons for the differences; integrates relevant examples, geographical information and sources where appropriate; presents a sustained, logical and cohesive response using appropriate geographical information, ideas, terms and concepts. (Lower bands: 13-16 well-developed; 9-12 sound with some reasons; 5-8 some knowledge, may refer to examples; 1-4 basic understanding.) Official 'answers could include' guidance: Reference to one type of ecosystem at two different locations that can be in Australia or overseas, for example Great Barrier Reef and New Caledonia Coral Reef, or Florida Everglades wetland ecosystem and Okavango Delta wetland ecosystem. Details of management strategies at each location such as: national park designation, landuse zoning, legislation, reforestation, fishing quotas, landuse agreements with Indigenous Peoples, closed seasons, wildlife breeding programs. Reasons for differences in ecosystem management could include: economic factors, politics, sociocultural factors (such as level of development, cultures, Indigenous Peoples' practices, ideology, nature of the economy, land ownership, business interests and government policy). Responses may refer to differences in complexity, enforcement, monitoring, compliance, and the success of management strategies at each location due to the influences identified."}, {"id": "cssa2025_q22", "source": "CSSA 2025 Trial", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [26], "marks": 20, "directive": "Assess", "question": "Assess the challenges facing rural and urban places.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (20 marks, band descriptors): Demonstrates a comprehensive knowledge and understanding of rural and urban places, makes informed judgements of the challenges facing rural and urban places, integrates relevant examples, geographical information and sources in the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate to support the response, presents a sustained, logical and cohesive response using appropriate geographical information, ideas, terms and concepts - 17-20 marks; Demonstrates well developed knowledge and understanding of rural and urban places, makes judgements of the challenges facing rural and urban places, refers relevant examples, geographical information and sources in the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate, presents a logical and cohesive response - 13-16 marks; Demonstrates a sound understanding of rural and urban places, provides some judgement of the challenges facing rural and urban places, refers to examples, geographical information and sources in the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate, presents a structured response using appropriate geographical information - 9-12 marks; Demonstrates some knowledge and understanding of the challenges of rural and urban places, may refer to examples, geographical information and/or sources in the Stimulus Booklet, uses some geographical information - 5-8 marks; Demonstrates a basic understanding of challenges in rural and urban places - 1-4 marks. Answers could include: Rural Challenges - environmental stress: droughts, floods, famines, bushfires, desertification; water supply and access: increased populations require more water access; employment issues: lack of workforce, rural/urban migration, increased mechanisation. Urban Challenges - access to affordable housing: high levels of mortgage stress in developed places such as New York; employment issues in developing countries: high levels of rural urban migration create employment issues within urban places such as Mumbai and large informal sectors develop which create service and infrastructure issues as result of no taxation; traffic congestion: large urban areas often have major challenges with traffic congestion as a result of high population levels and large-scale car ownership and road infrastructure not keeping up with population growth such as Los Angeles and Mexico City."}, {"id": "indep2025_q23", "source": "Independent Trial 2025", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [37], "marks": 20, "directive": "Assess", "question": "Assess the social, economic and environmental changes that have influenced the nature of ONE place within a larger urban settlement.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (17-20 marks): Demonstrates a comprehensive understanding of ONE place within a larger urban settlement; makes a clear judgement of the impact of the social, economic and environmental changes on the nature of ONE place; integrates relevant case studies, illustrative examples and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate; presents a sustained, logical and cohesive response using appropriate geographical information, ideas, terms and concepts. Lower bands (13-16, 9-12, 5-8, 1-4) require progressively less developed understanding, judgement and use of case study material. Sample answer: Green Square (GS), a precinct composed of five suburbs: Alexandria, Zetland, Waterloo, Rosebery and Beaconsfield is located 3.5 km from Sydney's CBD and 4 km from the airport. The dynamic of urban renewal has significantly shaped the suburb since the 1970s and, thus, the area has undergone significant social, economic and environmental change, altering its nature from a manufacturing centre to a world-class mixed-use precinct. As a result of the Building Better Cities project of the 1990s, GS is currently undergoing urban renewal, which is the redevelopment of an urban area involving a change in its functional role. The change in the economic nature and character of GS is evident in its changing economy and investment. The $13 billion project is at the forefront of economic change in Sydney; by 2036 there will be an additional 22 000 jobs added to GS, with a focus on service-based industries linked with the CBD, exhibiting a shift from the historically industrial site. These jobs will contribute to the projected $540 million that GS will provide to the City of Sydney's economy. GS will produce 36 000 more houses to provide tenure for 66 000 more individuals, combatting the shortage of dwellings that are predicted to emerge as residential land supply falls 789 000 below the expected population rise of 2030. The average costs of one-bedroom apartments ($800 000) and townhouses ($1.5m) are significantly higher than Sydney averages. Thus, residents of GS are of high socioeconomic status, evidenced by the median weekly income of more than $2000 (ABS). Problematically, however, due to 88% of the workforce currently being employed by a firm of 20 or less individuals (City of Sydney, 2023), as well as the economy's isolation 4 km away from the CBD, long-term capacity constraints threaten the continued economic restructuring and development of the urban renewal project. Whilst there are limitations on its continued growth and restructuring, there has been a distinct shift from the historical industries of manufacturing and distribution to one of service-based and commercial services. Urban renewal has altered the nature of the region dramatically, evidenced in the influence of urban renewal on GS offering employment, housing and sustainable suburban living. According to Observatory Hill EEC, it is 30% less expensive to develop brownfield growth through urban renewal, and thus, GS, being previously left to decay, has become a significant response to Sydney's rising population, predicted to reach 8 million by 2050. Stretching over 278 hectares, the development of GS has increased social sustainability through sympathetic infill, with the creation of the Joynton Avenue Creative Centre and Gunyama Swimming Pool. The City of Sydney states that these urban burgeons are 'cultural precincts that promise to invigorate the heart of the growing neighbourhood' improving cultural density through public place activation. More so, the rise of YUPS, SINKS and DINKS has greatly altered the nature of the region. The SMH estimates that citizens aged between 23-33 are wanting to live closer to areas of economic and cultural activity in single bed apartment blocks, reinforcing the construction of the OVO and Infinity by Crown Group buildings, which are enhancing GS's streetscape and providing luxury utilities such as the infinity heated pool, gymnasiums, cinemas and kitchen facilities. Coincidentally, as a result of GS's location 4 km from the CBD, connected through 1 train stop, and 4 km from the airport, GS will not only reinforce Sydney's status as a secondary world city (Redefining Global Cities Report 2016) but also ensure liveability amidst emerging and adaptive sociocultural lifestyles. Thus, the impact of urban renewal on GS has seen a shift in the demographic and cultural landscape from the historical norms of the suburb and is, therefore, significant in shaping its nature. Under Sydney's urban renewal scheme, public and private developers are closely collaborating along with environmental officials to ensure that GS becomes a powerhouse of ecological sustainability within Sydney, moving away from its industrial history. As prefaced on the City of Sydney's 'Planning Considerations', and under the operations of Landcom and Mirvac, GSQ hopes to achieve 'the highest rating of sustainability in the Green Star Communities Rating tool' (Clover Moore), utilising 'eco-architecture' and sustainable infrastructure to create the eco-centric 'Drying Green' park and the 'GS Water Building'; which harvests and purifies 900 kL of stormwater every day at full capacity. Furthermore, low carbon energy systems and bike and walking routes are ensuring that urban sprawl is reduced and that dependencies on car ownership are decreased, thus reducing the city's carbon footprint. Additionally, GS features one of Australia's largest urban stormwater recycling schemes - a new 2 km underground flood mitigation system from Epsom Road in Zetland to Shea's Creek channel in Alexandria. Due to the suburb's geographical location, sitting on a floodplain, Green Square's ecologically sustainable drainage has significantly reduced the risk of flooding in the town centre, allowing recycled water to be used by 61 000 residents across the region for washing clothes, flushing toilets and vitalising parks and gardens. Despite costing $140 million, the drainage system has been largely beneficial, both winning the Infrastructure Project Innovation Award for NSW at the 2019 NSW Water Awards and helping shape the suburb as an exemplar for 'green living'. Urban renewal has successfully shaped the ecological sustainability of GS, aligning it with the future trends of the city of Sydney and demonstrating the impact that urban dynamics of change can have on the future trends and ecological sustainability of a suburb."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_06", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [8], "marks": 20, "directive": "Examine", "question": "Examine the changing nature, spatial patterns and future directions of global tourism, with reference to examples you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n17-20 marks - Provides a comprehensive and well-supported examination of the changing nature, spatial patterns and future directions of global tourism, integrating specific, accurate case study evidence throughout\n13-16 marks - Provides a well-supported examination addressing most of nature, spatial patterns and future directions, with generally accurate case study evidence\n9-12 marks - Provides some explanation of aspects of global tourism's nature, spatial patterns and/or future directions, with limited case study evidence\n5-8 marks - Describes global tourism with limited reference to nature, spatial patterns or future directions\n1-4 marks - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer (approx. 380 words): Global tourism has changed substantially in nature over recent decades, shifting from mass, package-style holidays towards more individualised, experience-based and sustainability-conscious travel. This is visible at Kiama, NSW, where tourism has evolved from simple day-trip sightseeing around the Blowhole towards a broader visitor economy built on heritage precincts, food and nature-based experiences, supported by Kiama Council's growing use of technology (including exploring AI for customer service and planning) and continued investment in water conservation and waste management to handle rising visitor numbers sustainably.\n\nSpatial patterns in global tourism remain strongly uneven, concentrated around coastal, natural and alpine attractions and major transport corridors. At a national scale, Kiama's coastal location within easy reach of Sydney and Wollongong produces a pattern of short-stay, high-frequency visitation, while Thredbo Alpine Resort's more remote alpine location in Kosciuszko National Park produces a smaller number of longer, seasonally concentrated stays tied to the snow season. Thredbo's response of diversifying into summer activities such as mountain biking, hiking and fishing, and hosting over 100 annual events, illustrates a broader global trend of resorts extending their tourist season and spatial drawing area beyond a single peak period.\n\nFuture directions for both destinations point towards deeper integration of sustainability into tourism's core offering rather than treating it as an add-on. Thredbo's Gold EarthCheck certification, 100% renewable energy operation, closed-loop organic recycling, and partnership with Greenfleet (offsetting over 12,450 tonnes of carbon emissions through planting roughly 45,000 native trees) all position it as a 'green destination' aimed at attracting increasingly eco-conscious travellers - a market segment expected to keep growing. Kiama's future direction is likely to focus on managing growth so that increasing visitor numbers do not compromise the natural attractions (threatened/endangered species habitat, coastal ecosystems) that tourism itself depends on, consistent with Kiama Council's stated commitment to sustainable development. Together, these examples suggest global tourism's future lies less in maximising visitor volume and more in maximising the quality, sustainability and year-round spread of visitation."}, {"id": "ppa_t1_12", "source": "Past Paper Analysis (adapted)", "topicNumber": 1, "dotPoints": [11], "marks": 20, "directive": "Critically analyse", "question": "Critically analyse ONE strategy for achieving sustainability in global tourism, with reference to Thredbo Alpine Resort.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria:\n17-20 marks - Provides a sophisticated, well-substantiated critical analysis of the strategy, weighing its strengths against limitations and using precise case study evidence throughout\n13-16 marks - Provides a well-supported analysis of the strategy with some critical judgement and generally accurate evidence\n9-12 marks - Describes the strategy with limited critical judgement, some case study evidence\n5-8 marks - Describes the strategy with limited case study evidence\n1-4 marks - Provides some relevant information\n\nSample answer (approx. 360 words): Thredbo Alpine Resort's environmental sustainability strategy is a genuinely multi-pronged response to the ecological risks alpine tourism creates, and it succeeds on several measures. Environmentally, the resort operates on 100% renewable energy, uses SNOWsat technology to make snow grooming more energy- and water-efficient, and runs closed-loop organic recycling that turns waste into compost rather than sending it to landfill. Its partnership with Greenfleet, which has offset over 12,450 tonnes of carbon emissions by planting roughly 45,000 native trees, together with Gold EarthCheck certification, gives the strategy external verification rather than relying on the resort's own claims - a genuine strength, since greenwashing is a common criticism of tourism sustainability strategies elsewhere. The strategy also restores alpine habitat within Kosciuszko National Park, directly addressing biodiversity loss rather than only carbon emissions.\n\nEconomically and socially, the strategy is reinforced by diversifying income beyond winter skiing into mountain biking, hiking and fishing, and hosting over 100 annual events; this both reduces the resort's vulnerability to poor snow seasons (a growing risk under climate change) and provides more consistent, year-round local employment, addressing the seasonal job insecurity common in alpine tourism. Positioning as a 'green destination' also appears to be commercially effective, attracting eco-conscious travellers as a distinct market segment.\n\nHowever, the strategy has limitations worth weighing critically. Alpine tourism is inherently reliant on a shrinking snow season under climate change, meaning even a highly sustainable resort operates within an activity whose long-term physical basis is under threat - sustainability initiatives address the resort's own footprint but cannot resolve this deeper structural vulnerability. There is also a question of scale: tree planting and habitat restoration, while valuable, are modest compared to the scale of emissions from visitor transport to and from the resort, which the strategy does not appear to directly address. Overall, Thredbo's strategy is a strong, well-evidenced example of tourism sustainability management, but its success should be understood as significantly reducing the resort's own operational impact rather than resolving the sector's broader sustainability challenges."}, {"id": "trial2026-w16", "source": "2026 Trial", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [33], "marks": 20, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "Sustainable management of places requires the integration of environmental, economic and social considerations at a range of scales.\n\nWith reference to ONE rural and ONE urban place you have studied, evaluate the effectiveness of strategies used to achieve sustainable management.", "markingGuideline": "Criteria (extended-response marking bands):\n17\u201320 marks \u2013 Demonstrates comprehensive knowledge and understanding of the sustainable management of ONE rural and ONE urban place; evaluates extensively the effectiveness of strategies used for sustainable management; integrates relevant case studies, illustrative examples and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate; presents a sustained, logical and cohesive response using appropriate geographical information, terms and concepts.\n13\u201316 marks \u2013 Demonstrates well-developed knowledge of the sustainable management of ONE rural and ONE urban place; assesses the effectiveness of strategies used for sustainable management; refers to relevant case studies, illustrative examples and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate; presents a logical response using appropriate geographical information and terms.\n9\u201312 marks \u2013 Demonstrates sound knowledge and understanding of the sustainable management of ONE rural and ONE urban place; provides some judgement of the effectiveness of strategies used for sustainable management; refers to case studies, illustrative examples and the Stimulus Booklet where appropriate; presents a structured response using appropriate geographical information.\n5\u20138 marks \u2013 Demonstrates basic knowledge and understanding of the sustainable management of a rural OR an urban place; provides an outline of sustainable management strategies; may refer to case studies, illustrative examples and/or the Stimulus Booklet; uses some geographical information.\n1\u20134 marks \u2013 Demonstrates some basic knowledge of rural or urban places.\n\nAnswers could include:\nBarangaroo (urban) \u2013 Environmental strategies: Solar power, green roofs, louvre shading and Water-Sensitive Urban Design (WSUD) reduce the precinct's environmental impact, supporting its carbon-neutral design; ~2.5 hectares of native sandstone headland habitat ecologically restored at Barangaroo Reserve, reintroducing endemic vegetation and creating public swimming access at Marrinawi Cove; transformed a contaminated industrial waterfront into a carbon-neutral precinct \u2014 measurable, evidence-based environmental outcomes.\nBarangaroo (urban) \u2013 Social strategies: First Nations cultural design incorporated into public space, including the Wulugul Walk and Girra Girra Steps, developed with reference to Cammeraygal heritage; public open space intended to be accessible to all Sydneysiders; however, redevelopment from a working-class industrial waterfront into a luxury precinct (Crown Sydney, global HQs including HSBC, Meta and Westpac) has raised rents in nearby Millers Point and The Rocks, displacing long-term, lower-income residents \u2014 a clear equity limitation.\nBarangaroo (urban) \u2013 Economic strategies: Disused industrial land transformed into one of Sydney's premier commercial hubs, generating substantial tax revenue and employment; multinational tenants connect Sydney to over 143 countries, reinforcing global economic significance.\nBerry (rural) \u2013 Environmental strategies: Riparian restoration along Broughton Creek and weed eradication (lantana, privet) improve local ecosystem health; Boongaree Nature Play Park integrates green space into the village.\nBerry (rural) \u2013 Economic strategies: Berry Chamber of Commerce and monthly farmers' markets have diversified the local economy beyond dairy farming into tourism, boutique retail and artisan food production; Berry Bypass removed through-traffic and repositioned Berry as a destination town, proving economically resilient as global dairy markets have declined.\nBerry (rural) \u2013 Social strategies: Community events (Berry Show) and heritage protections under the Princess Street Heritage Conservation Area preserve local identity and cultural continuity; Shoalhaven 2035 Community Strategic Plan provides a formal government framework addressing safety, culture and sport; however, popularity as a \u201cSea Change\u201d destination has driven rising property prices, risking the displacement of long-term residents and agricultural users \u2014 echoing Barangaroo's equity concerns.\nJudgements could include: Both places show sustainable management strategies are most effective for environmental and economic goals, where measurable, funded interventions (habitat restoration, economic diversification, green infrastructure) have delivered clear outcomes in both urban and rural contexts. Social sustainability is where both places show the greatest limitations: rising property values, gentrification and displacement affect both Barangaroo and Berry despite their very different scales and contexts. This suggests market-driven redevelopment and destination branding \u2014 even paired with strong environmental design \u2014 can undermine social equity unless explicitly managed alongside it. Overall, effective sustainable management requires an integrated, place-specific approach across all three pillars; inequality in access to the benefits of sustainability remains the most persistent challenge in both rural and urban Australian places."}, {"id": "adapt2-t2-13", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 2, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 20, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "Evaluate the effectiveness of traditional and contemporary management strategies used to protect TWO ecosystems at risk that you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Band 5 (17-20 marks): sustained, well-structured evaluation of traditional AND contemporary strategies across TWO ecosystems, integrating specific named case-study evidence (statistics, project names, outcomes) throughout, and reaching a well-substantiated overall judgement with explicit criteria.\nBand 4 (13-16 marks): sound evaluation of strategies for two ecosystems with good specific detail, though integration or judgement may be less consistent.\nBand 3 (9-12 marks): describes strategies for one or two ecosystems with some evaluation, but relies more on description than judgement.\nBand 2 (5-8 marks): general description of ecosystem management with limited specific detail or case-study evidence.\nBand 1 (1-4 marks): isolated or irrelevant statements.\n\nSample approach: structure around two contrasting ecosystems (e.g. a reef ecosystem and a wetland or rainforest ecosystem), for each naming one traditional and one contemporary strategy, using statistics/outcomes to judge effectiveness, and concluding with a comparative judgement about which combinations of strategies proved most effective and why."}, {"id": "adapt2-t3-13", "source": "Adapted from past HSC (old syllabus)", "topicNumber": 3, "dotPoints": [], "marks": 20, "directive": "Evaluate", "question": "Evaluate the effectiveness of strategies used to manage the impacts of urban change on the ecological sustainability of a large urban place you have studied.", "markingGuideline": "Band 5 (17-20 marks): sustained, well-structured evaluation of multiple named strategies (e.g. transport, energy, green space, density planning), integrating specific statistics/evidence throughout, and reaching a well-substantiated overall judgement with explicit criteria and at least one acknowledged limitation.\nBand 4 (13-16 marks): sound evaluation with good specific detail, though integration or judgement may be less consistent.\nBand 3 (9-12 marks): describes strategies with some evaluation, relying more on description than judgement.\nBand 2 (5-8 marks): general description with limited specific detail or evidence.\nBand 1 (1-4 marks): isolated or irrelevant statements.\n\nSample approach: e.g. evaluate a named urban place's public transport investment, renewable energy targets, and green-space/urban-forest strategy using measurable outcomes (mode share change, emissions data, canopy cover), concluding which strategies have been most effective and identifying at least one persistent limitation (funding, pace, equity of access)."}];